          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which PK/PD index best predicts the clinical efficacy of aminoglycosides against</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which PK/PD index best predicts the clinical efficacy of aminoglycosides against gram-negative organisms?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Time above MIC (T&gt;MIC)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>T&gt;MIC predicts efficacy for time-dependent killers like β-lactams, not for concentration-dependent agents such as aminoglycosides.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>AUC/MIC ratio</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Aminoglycosides are concentration-dependent killers; the AUC/MIC target for gram-negatives is &gt;125, reflecting total drug exposure relative to the organism's susceptibility.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Trough-to-MIC ratio</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Trough levels are monitored for aminoglycoside toxicity (nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity), but they do not predict bactericidal efficacy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Steady-state volume of distribution</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Volume of distribution describes how a drug distributes across body compartments — it is a pharmacokinetic parameter, not a predictor of antimicrobial efficacy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the PK/PD indices: AUC/MIC predicts efficacy for concentration-dependent killers (aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones), while T>MIC predicts efficacy for time-dependent killers (β-lactams, vancomycin)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Understanding that aminoglycosides are concentration-dependent killers guided by AUC/MIC is essential for dosing rationale — it justifies once-daily high-dose (extended-interval) aminoglycoside regimens."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
