          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In WHO multi-drug therapy for leprosy, which drug is the bactericidal cornerston</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In WHO multi-drug therapy for leprosy, which drug is the bactericidal cornerstone given as a supervised monthly dose?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rifampicin</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Rifampicin is the most potent bactericidal agent against M. leprae and is given as a single supervised monthly dose of 600 mg — the cornerstone of WHO MDT.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Dapsone</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Dapsone is bacteriostatic (inhibits folate synthesis) and is given as a daily self-administered dose of 100 mg — it is not the supervised monthly bactericidal agent.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Clofazimine</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Clofazimine is bacteriostatic with anti-inflammatory properties. While it has a supervised monthly component (300 mg), it also requires daily dosing (50 mg) and is used only in multibacillary leprosy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ofloxacin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ofloxacin is a second-line alternative used when rifampicin cannot be tolerated. It is bactericidal but is not part of the standard first-line MDT regimen.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: Rifampicin (600 mg supervised monthly) is the bactericidal cornerstone. Dapsone (daily, bacteriostatic) and Clofazimine (daily + monthly, bacteriostatic and anti-inflammatory) complete the MDT regimen."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Rifampicin's powerful bactericidal action against M. leprae is why it is given under direct observation — ensuring compliance with the single most important drug in the regimen."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
