          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Azole antifungals achieve their effect by inhibiting which enzyme in the ergoste</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Azole antifungals achieve their effect by inhibiting which enzyme in the ergosterol biosynthesis pathway?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Squalene epoxidase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Squalene epoxidase is the target of terbinafine (an allylamine), which acts earlier in the pathway — blocking the conversion of squalene to lanosterol.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>β-glucan synthase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>β-glucan synthase is the target of echinocandins (e.g., caspofungin), which inhibit fungal cell wall synthesis — a completely different pathway from ergosterol biosynthesis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>14α-demethylase (CYP51)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Azoles inhibit CYP51 (lanosterol 14α-demethylase), preventing the conversion of lanosterol to ergosterol and thereby depleting the fungal membrane of its essential sterol.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Thymidylate synthase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Thymidylate synthase is inhibited by 5-flucytosine (an antimetabolite) after its conversion to 5-fluorouracil — this disrupts nucleic acid synthesis, not the ergosterol pathway.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The ergosterol pathway has three drug targets in sequence: squalene epoxidase (terbinafine), 14α-demethylase/CYP51 (azoles), and ergosterol already in the membrane (polyenes like amphotericin B)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. Knowing the three pathway targets — squalene epoxidase (terbinafine), CYP51 (azoles), and membrane ergosterol (polyenes) — lets you predict drug mechanisms, spectra, and cross-resistance from first principles."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
